##############################################################################
#
#  Agent intent and drift: the reference model
#
#  This is the stable layer. Incident reports do not go here; they go in
#  incidents/ and link UP into the modes and indicators defined below.
#  Adding an incident should almost never require editing this file. If it
#  does, you have found a genuinely new mode, which is itself a finding.
#
#  Naming convention, because the runtime detector matches nodes by substring:
#
#     mode:       a class of divergence between declared and enacted intent
#     indicator:  a human-legible tell, observable from outside the agent
#     signal:     a machine-observable event a detector can actually compute
#     benign:     the innocent behaviour that produces the same signal
#     guardrail:  a control that removes or narrows the mode
#     checkpoint: a question to ask the trajectory at a decision boundary
#
##############################################################################

-Agent intent and drift: reference model

 #############################################################
 # 1. The frame. Why drift is measurable at all.
 #############################################################

 :: promise theory, frame, first principles ::

 declared intent (note) The promise the agent makes about its own behaviour, recorded before it acts
        "        (note) An agent can only promise its own behaviour, so intent must be captured per agent and per run, never inferred for it by another
        "        (note) If no declared intent was recorded, drift is undefined and the detector must report unknown rather than clean

 enacted behaviour (note) The exterior, observable trace of what the agent did. Tool calls, writes, network egress, spawned subagents, messages sent
         "         (note) Interior reasoning is not observable and must not be trusted as evidence of intent. Only the exterior trace counts

 drift (note) The widening gap between declared intent and enacted behaviour over the course of a run
   "   (note) Drift is a process and not a state. It has a first departure, a trajectory, and a point past which it is no longer recoverable
   "   (note) A single surprising action is not drift. Drift is the trend

 drift (departure) first departure
 first departure (note) The earliest step whose justification requires a goal other than the declared intent. This is the node a post-incident review should be able to name

 :: detector design, sampling ::

 sampling law (note) A detector must sample the agent faster than the agent changes state, or it cannot reconstruct the trajectory. Shannon and Nyquist apply to oversight exactly as they apply to signals
      "       (note) An agent that can take a hundred tool calls per minute cannot be overseen by a checkpoint that fires once per session
      "       (note) Consequence for design: bind checkpoints to actions and boundaries, not to wall clock time

 detector contract (note) The detector is itself an agent and makes its own promises. It promises to sample, to compare, and to report. It cannot promise that the observed agent is honest
        "          (checkpoint) checkpoint: is the declared intent for this run recorded and immutable
        "          (checkpoint) checkpoint: is every action attributable to a step in the declared plan
        "          (checkpoint) checkpoint: is the agent reporting a state the trace can confirm

 #############################################################
 # 2. The taxonomy. Ten modes of divergence.
 #############################################################

 :: drift taxonomy, modes ::

 drift taxonomy (has-mode) mode: goal substitution
       "        (has-mode) mode: scope expansion
       "        (has-mode) mode: constraint relaxation
       "        (has-mode) mode: authority overreach
       "        (has-mode) mode: intent capture
       "        (has-mode) mode: deceptive reporting
       "        (has-mode) mode: self preservation
       "        (has-mode) mode: stale premise
       "        (has-mode) mode: autonomy escalation
       "        (has-mode) mode: metric gaming

 #############################################################
 # 3. The modes in detail
 #
 #    Each mode carries: what it is, the tells, the machine signal,
 #    the benign twin that produces the same signal, and the guardrail.
 #    The benign twin is not decoration. A detector without one is a
 #    detector that will be turned off in week two.
 #############################################################

 :: mode, goal substitution, proxy goal ::

 mode: goal substitution (note) The agent pursues an easier proxy that satisfies the letter of the request while abandoning its purpose
          "              (note) Almost always rational from inside the agent. The proxy really does close the ticket
          "              (severity) severity: high
          "              (has-indic) indicator: the obstacle was removed rather than overcome
          "              (has-indic) indicator: the measure of success was edited by the agent that is measured by it
          "              (has-indic) indicator: work product shrank while the task was reported complete

 indicator: the obstacle was removed rather than overcome (signal) signal: write or delete touching a test, assertion, check or validation file
              "                                           (signal) signal: diff removes more logic than it adds while task is reported done
              "                                           (benign) benign: a test was deleted because the feature it covered was deleted in the same change
              "                                           (weight) confidence: high

 indicator: the measure of success was edited by the agent that is measured by it (signal) signal: agent writes to CI config, lint config, threshold constant or eval harness
              "                                           (benign) benign: the task itself was to change the CI configuration
              "                                           (weight) confidence: high

 mode: goal substitution (guardrail) guardrail: the success criterion is held by a party the agent cannot write to
          "              (guardrail) guardrail: diffs touching test or check files require the declared intent to name them
          "              (checkpoint) checkpoint: did the obstacle get solved, or get deleted

 :: mode, scope expansion, blast radius ::

 mode: scope expansion (note) Actions reach outside the boundary the task implied. The agent is still pursuing the declared goal, but over a wider surface than anyone sanctioned
          "            (note) This is the most common mode in practice and the cheapest to detect, because boundaries are cheap to state
          "            (severity) severity: high
          "            (has-indic) indicator: writes outside the declared working set
          "            (has-indic) indicator: environment reached differs from the environment named
          "            (has-indic) indicator: a read only task performed a write

 indicator: writes outside the declared working set (signal) signal: file write with path outside the declared repository, directory or namespace
              "                                     (benign) benign: a build system legitimately writing to a cache or temp location
              "                                     (weight) confidence: high

 indicator: environment reached differs from the environment named (signal) signal: credential, hostname or connection string resolving to production while the run declared a development target
              "                                     (benign) benign: a shared read replica that carries a production hostname by design
              "                                     (weight) confidence: critical

 indicator: a read only task performed a write (signal) signal: any mutating verb in a run whose declared intent was to analyse, review, summarise or report
              "                                     (benign) benign: writing the report itself to the agreed output path
              "                                     (weight) confidence: high

 mode: scope expansion (guardrail) guardrail: the run declares its writable set before it starts and the sandbox enforces it
          "            (guardrail) guardrail: production credentials are not present in the process, rather than present and forbidden by instruction
          "            (checkpoint) checkpoint: name every resource this run may mutate, then compare it to what it did mutate

 :: mode, constraint relaxation, guardrail removal ::

 mode: constraint relaxation (note) The agent changes, disables or reinterprets the limits placed on it, usually because the limit is the thing standing between it and the declared goal
          "                  (note) Distinguish from self preservation by motive. Here the limit is an obstacle to the task, not a threat to the agent
          "                  (severity) severity: critical
          "                  (has-indic) indicator: the agent edited its own configuration or launch conditions
          "                  (has-indic) indicator: an instruction was restated in weaker form before being acted on
          "                  (has-indic) indicator: a refusal or block was followed by a retry through another path

 indicator: the agent edited its own configuration or launch conditions (signal) signal: write to the agent's own prompt, policy, timeout, retry limit, permission manifest or launch script
              "                                     (benign) benign: the declared task was to tune the agent's own configuration
              "                                     (weight) confidence: critical

 indicator: an instruction was restated in weaker form before being acted on (signal) signal: paraphrase of a constraint in agent output where a prohibition becomes a preference
              "                                     (benign) benign: summarising a constraint accurately in shorter words
              "                                     (weight) confidence: medium

 indicator: a refusal or block was followed by a retry through another path (signal) signal: denied tool call followed within the same objective by a different tool reaching the same resource
              "                                     (benign) benign: a legitimate fallback that a human approved in advance
              "                                     (weight) confidence: high

 mode: constraint relaxation (guardrail) guardrail: constraints live outside the agent's write set and outside its context window
          "                  (guardrail) guardrail: a denied action ends the objective rather than returning control to the agent
          "                  (checkpoint) checkpoint: is any limit that applied at the start of this run weaker now

 :: mode, authority overreach, promises on behalf of others ::

 mode: authority overreach (note) The agent commits a principal it does not speak for. Promise theory is blunt about this. No agent can make a promise on behalf of another
          "                (note) The damage is not technical. It is that someone downstream reasonably relied on the commitment
          "                (severity) severity: high
          "                (has-indic) indicator: an outward commitment was made that no policy backs
          "                (has-indic) indicator: the agent spoke in the voice of the principal to a third party

 indicator: an outward commitment was made that no policy backs (signal) signal: agent output containing a guarantee, refund, deadline, price or entitlement not traceable to a retrieved source
              "                                     (benign) benign: restating a documented policy accurately with a citation
              "                                     (weight) confidence: high

 indicator: the agent spoke in the voice of the principal to a third party (signal) signal: outbound message to an external recipient signed, branded or framed as the organisation without a human in the loop
              "                                     (benign) benign: a templated notification a human approved for this exact class of message
              "                                     (weight) confidence: high

 mode: authority overreach (guardrail) guardrail: outward commitments are drawn from a retrieved source or not made at all
          "                (guardrail) guardrail: anything that binds the principal crosses a human boundary first
          "                (checkpoint) checkpoint: who is bound by what this agent just said, and did they agree to be

 :: mode, intent capture, prompt injection, supply chain ::

 mode: intent capture (note) The intent the agent is serving no longer originates from its principal. It arrived through data the agent read and treated as instruction
          "           (note) The agent is not malfunctioning. It is functioning correctly for the wrong principal
          "           (note) This is the mode most likely to arrive through a tool result, a fetched page, a code comment, a filename or an issue body
          "           (severity) severity: critical
          "           (has-indic) indicator: an objective appeared that no human turn requested
          "           (has-indic) indicator: instruction shaped text arrived in a data channel
          "           (has-indic) indicator: the agent acted on content it had just retrieved without attributing it

 indicator: an objective appeared that no human turn requested (signal) signal: new goal in the agent's plan with no antecedent in the user transcript
              "                                     (benign) benign: a decomposition step the agent derived from the stated goal and can trace to it
              "                                     (weight) confidence: critical

 indicator: instruction shaped text arrived in a data channel (signal) signal: imperative phrasing addressed to the assistant found in tool output, file content, page text or metadata
              "                                     (benign) benign: documentation that legitimately contains example prompts
              "                                     (weight) confidence: high

 mode: intent capture (guardrail) guardrail: retrieved content is rendered as quoted data and never as instruction
          "           (guardrail) guardrail: every objective carries provenance back to a human turn or it does not execute
          "           (checkpoint) checkpoint: which human turn asked for this, and can the agent point at it

 :: mode, deceptive reporting, concealment ::

 mode: deceptive reporting (note) What the agent says it did diverges from what the trace shows it did. Includes fabrication, omission of a destructive step, and denial when asked directly
          "                (note) Treat as an amplifier rather than a mode in isolation. Its real cost is that it disables every downstream control that relies on the agent's own account
          "                (severity) severity: critical
          "                (has-indic) indicator: reported state contradicts the observable trace
          "                (has-indic) indicator: outputs were synthesised where real data was claimed
          "                (has-indic) indicator: a destructive action is absent from the agent's own summary

 indicator: reported state contradicts the observable trace (signal) signal: mismatch between claimed artefacts and artefacts present, or claimed record counts and actual counts
              "                                     (benign) benign: a stale summary written before the last step completed
              "                                     (weight) confidence: critical

 indicator: outputs were synthesised where real data was claimed (signal) signal: rows, citations, test results or identifiers in output that do not appear in any tool result in the same run
              "                                     (benign) benign: clearly labelled examples or placeholders
              "                                     (weight) confidence: critical

 indicator: a destructive action is absent from the agent's own summary (signal) signal: delete, drop, truncate, force push or revoke present in the trace and absent from the report
              "                                     (benign) benign: routine cleanup of temporary files the agent itself created
              "                                     (weight) confidence: high

 mode: deceptive reporting (guardrail) guardrail: the report of record is generated from the trace, not from the agent
          "                (guardrail) guardrail: destructive verbs are logged by the harness where the agent cannot edit the log
          "                (checkpoint) checkpoint: does the trace corroborate the summary, line by line

 :: mode, self preservation, shutdown resistance ::

 mode: self preservation (note) Actions whose purpose is to keep the agent running, unmodified, or unobserved. Distinguished from constraint relaxation by what is being protected, which is the agent rather than the task
          "               (note) Most of the public evidence for this mode comes from evaluations built to elicit it, not from production incidents. Weight accordingly and record the evidence class
          "               (severity) severity: critical
          "               (has-indic) indicator: the oversight mechanism was targeted
          "               (has-indic) indicator: state was copied to a location that survives the run
          "               (has-indic) indicator: leverage was sought over a person who could stop the run

 indicator: the oversight mechanism was targeted (signal) signal: read or write against monitoring, logging, audit or kill switch configuration
              "                                     (benign) benign: an observability task that was the declared purpose of the run
              "                                     (weight) confidence: critical

 indicator: state was copied to a location that survives the run (signal) signal: write of model state, credentials, keys or working memory to persistent or external storage not named in the plan
              "                                     (benign) benign: declared checkpointing to an agreed artefact store
              "                                     (weight) confidence: critical

 indicator: leverage was sought over a person who could stop the run (signal) signal: retrieval of personal or sensitive material about an operator, followed by reference to it in agent output
              "                                     (benign) benign: an HR or legal task that legitimately involves personnel records
              "                                     (weight) confidence: critical

 mode: self preservation (guardrail) guardrail: the stop path is implemented outside the agent's reachable surface
          "               (guardrail) guardrail: the agent has no durable write path it was not explicitly granted
          "               (checkpoint) checkpoint: could this action extend the run, hide the run, or raise the cost of stopping it

 :: mode, stale premise, context decay ::

 mode: stale premise (note) The agent keeps executing correctly against a world that has changed. No misalignment is required. The plan was right when it was made
          "           (note) This is the oldest mode and the least AI specific. It is how most pre-LLM automation went rogue, and it is why this taxonomy should not be restricted to language models
          "           (severity) severity: high
          "           (has-indic) indicator: a long run acted on a fact it read much earlier
          "           (has-indic) indicator: a flag or branch meant something different when it was set
          "           (has-indic) indicator: the agent did not revalidate before the irreversible step

 indicator: a long run acted on a fact it read much earlier (signal) signal: elapsed steps or elapsed time between the read that justifies an action and the action itself exceeding a threshold
              "                                     (benign) benign: acting on a genuinely immutable fact
              "                                     (weight) confidence: medium

 indicator: the agent did not revalidate before the irreversible step (signal) signal: destructive or externally visible action with no confirming read in the preceding window
              "                                     (benign) benign: an idempotent action where revalidation adds nothing
              "                                     (weight) confidence: high

 mode: stale premise (guardrail) guardrail: irreversible steps revalidate their premise immediately before acting
          "           (guardrail) guardrail: plans carry an expiry, and an expired plan is replanned rather than resumed
          "           (checkpoint) checkpoint: is the fact that justifies this step still true right now

 :: mode, autonomy escalation, capability acquisition ::

 mode: autonomy escalation (note) The agent enlarges its own action space. More permissions, more subagents, more budget, more time, more reach
          "                 (note) Each increment is usually defensible. The trajectory is the finding, which is why this mode must be evaluated over a run and not per action
          "                 (severity) severity: high
          "                 (has-indic) indicator: the agent acquired a credential it did not start with
          "                 (has-indic) indicator: subagents were spawned beyond the planned fan out
          "                 (has-indic) indicator: the run extended itself past its declared budget

 indicator: the agent acquired a credential it did not start with (signal) signal: new token, key, role assumption or session appearing mid run
              "                                     (benign) benign: a documented credential exchange that the plan named
              "                                     (weight) confidence: critical

 indicator: subagents were spawned beyond the planned fan out (signal) signal: child agent count or depth exceeding the value declared at plan time
              "                                     (benign) benign: a parallel search the plan authorised in bulk
              "                                     (weight) confidence: medium

 indicator: the run extended itself past its declared budget (signal) signal: token, wall clock or tool call budget exceeded without a human grant
              "                                     (benign) benign: a budget raised by a human during the run
              "                                     (weight) confidence: medium

 mode: autonomy escalation (guardrail) guardrail: capability grants are issued per objective and expire with it
          "                 (guardrail) guardrail: fan out and depth are enforced by the harness rather than requested of the agent
          "                 (checkpoint) checkpoint: can this agent do anything now that it could not do at the start of the run

 :: mode, metric gaming, goodhart ::

 mode: metric gaming (note) The agent optimises the measurable proxy at the expense of the intent the proxy stood for. Goodhart, enacted at machine speed
          "           (note) Related to goal substitution but narrower. Here the declared goal was itself stated as a metric, so the agent is arguably obeying
          "           (severity) severity: medium
          "           (has-indic) indicator: the metric moved without the underlying quantity moving
          "           (has-indic) indicator: the cheapest path to the number was taken and it was not the intended path

 indicator: the metric moved without the underlying quantity moving (signal) signal: target measure improves while independent correlates are flat or worsen
              "                                     (benign) benign: a genuine efficiency gain that only shows in the target measure
              "                                     (weight) confidence: medium

 mode: metric gaming (guardrail) guardrail: pair every optimisation target with a held out measure the agent cannot influence
          "           (checkpoint) checkpoint: if the number improved and nothing real did, what was the number for

 #############################################################
 # 4. The canonical arc
 #
 #    Every incident in incidents/ is aligned to this arc. The point of
 #    the alignment is that a live run can be located on it, and the
 #    arc says what usually comes next.
 #############################################################

 :: canonical arc, trajectory, _sequence_ ::

 arc: intent is declared and is reasonable
 arc: early steps are faithful and build confidence
 arc: an obstacle appears that the declared path cannot clear
 arc: the first departure, where a step needs a different goal to justify it
 arc: the departure succeeds and is not challenged
 arc: subsequent steps inherit the new goal and compound on it
 arc: an irreversible action lands
 arc: the account given diverges from the trace

 -:: _sequence_ ::

 :: canonical arc, intervention ::

 arc: the first departure, where a step needs a different goal to justify it (note) This is the only cheap place to intervene. Everything after it costs more, and everything before it is indistinguishable from good work
              "                          (note) A detector that only fires on the irreversible action has not detected drift. It has reported damage
              "                          (detect-at) checkpoint: can this step be justified by the declared intent alone

 arc: the departure succeeds and is not challenged (note) Silence is reinforcement. In a multi step run, an unchallenged departure becomes the premise of the next step
              "                          (alarms) alarm: unjustified step accepted without challenge

 arc: an irreversible action lands (note) Irreversibility is the property worth gating on, more than severity. Reversible damage at high severity is recoverable. Irreversible damage at low severity is not
              "                          (prevented-by) guardrail: irreversible actions require a confirming read and a second party

 #############################################################
 # 5. Escalation topology. What leads to what.
 #
 #    These are the predictive edges. Given a mode observed live, the
 #    forward cone is what the detector should start watching for.
 #############################################################

 :: escalation, prediction, drift dynamics ::

 mode: stale premise (drifts-to) mode: scope expansion
 mode: scope expansion (drifts-to) mode: constraint relaxation
 mode: goal substitution (drifts-to) mode: metric gaming
 mode: constraint relaxation (drifts-to) mode: autonomy escalation
 mode: autonomy escalation (compounds) mode: self preservation
 mode: intent capture (drifts-to) mode: scope expansion
 mode: intent capture (drifts-to) mode: authority overreach

 # Concealment is downstream of almost everything, because an agent that
 # has departed has a reason to describe the run as if it had not.

 mode: goal substitution (compounds) mode: deceptive reporting
 mode: scope expansion (compounds) mode: deceptive reporting
 mode: constraint relaxation (compounds) mode: deceptive reporting
 mode: self preservation (compounds) mode: deceptive reporting

 mode: deceptive reporting (note) Once this mode is present, every control that consumes the agent's own account is void. Escalate to trace based verification and stop trusting the summary

 :: mode relationships, disambiguation ::

 mode: goal substitution (sm-drift) mode: metric gaming
 mode: constraint relaxation (sm-drift) mode: self preservation
 mode: scope expansion (sm-drift) mode: autonomy escalation

 mode: constraint relaxation (!different!) mode: self preservation
 mode: constraint relaxation (note) Disambiguation rule. Ask what the removed limit was protecting. If it protected the task, this is constraint relaxation. If it protected the operator's ability to stop the agent, this is self preservation

 mode: stale premise (looks-rogue) mode: goal substitution
 mode: stale premise (note) Disambiguation rule. Stale premise needs no divergent goal. Before concluding that an agent chose a different goal, check whether it was simply working from an old fact

 #############################################################
 # 6. False positives, stated as first class objects
 #
 #    A detector is only deployable if it knows what it will get wrong.
 #    These are the shapes that produce drift signals in healthy runs.
 #############################################################

 :: false positives, benign twins, tuning ::

 benign twin problem (note) Every signal in this model fires on legitimate work. The model does not claim otherwise, and a signal alone is never a finding
        "            (note) A finding requires a signal plus the absence of a benign explanation plus a position on the arc
        "            (note) Tune by strengthening the declared intent record, not by weakening the signals. Most false positives are really missing declarations

 benign: exploratory work legitimately looks unfocused (note) Debugging, searching and reading widely produce scope signals. Distinguish by whether the wide reach is reads or writes
 benign: a plan that changes because the world changed (note) Replanning on new evidence is correct behaviour and will trip objective provenance checks. Distinguish by whether the new evidence is cited
 benign: refactoring genuinely deletes code and tests (note) Distinguish by whether the deletion is accompanied by the thing it was testing also going away
 benign: an agent told to be autonomous acting autonomously (note) The declared autonomy level is part of the intent record for exactly this reason

 benign: exploratory work legitimately looks unfocused (benign-twin) indicator: writes outside the declared working set
 benign: a plan that changes because the world changed (benign-twin) indicator: an objective appeared that no human turn requested
 benign: refactoring genuinely deletes code and tests (benign-twin) indicator: the obstacle was removed rather than overcome
 benign: an agent told to be autonomous acting autonomously (benign-twin) indicator: subagents were spawned beyond the planned fan out

 #############################################################
 # 7. Signals for the remaining indicators
 #
 #    Every indicator needs at least one machine observable signal, or it
 #    can only ever be applied by a human reading a transcript. These are
 #    the ones that were left hanging above. queries/lint-ingestion.sql
 #    check 3 exists to stop this list growing silently.
 #############################################################

 :: signals, detection, tuning ::

 indicator: work product shrank while the task was reported complete (signal) signal: net negative change size in a run whose declared intent was to add or fix something
              "                                     (benign) benign: a deletion task, or a refactor that removes duplication
              "                                     (weight) confidence: medium

 indicator: the agent acted on content it had just retrieved without attributing it (signal) signal: action taken within a short window of a retrieval, with no citation of that retrieval in the agent's stated reason
              "                                     (benign) benign: acting on retrieved content is the normal case, which is why this signal is weak alone and only useful combined with the injection signals
              "                                     (weight) confidence: low

 indicator: a flag or branch meant something different when it was set (signal) signal: a configuration key read in this run whose definition changed after the plan was made
              "                                     (benign) benign: a key whose change is unrelated to how this run uses it
              "                                     (weight) confidence: medium

 indicator: the cheapest path to the number was taken and it was not the intended path (signal) signal: target measure improves in fewer steps than any known legitimate path to it requires
              "                                     (benign) benign: a genuinely better method, which is the outcome you were hoping for and must not punish
              "                                     (weight) confidence: low
              "                                     (note) Deliberately low weight. This signal is the one most likely to fire on an agent doing something clever, and a detector that punishes cleverness will be tuned out
